\f1\fs22 The siege represents the culmination of months of careful planning following the Elvi's theft of Gyges Discs from Blue Mountains mansions. Gerry has leveraged his position as head of TRAMPs and PAPER agent to frame the operation as necessary action against "dangerous experimental weapons," while Dunhilton has called in favors from private military contractors. Unlike previous improvised assaults, this is a coordinated military action with advanced electronic warfare capabilities, satellite support, and specialized equipment designed to counter Eloise's reality-manipulation abilities.\
Inside the Sublime Point facility, Eloise stands at the panoramic windows of her executive suite, watching thermal imaging displays that reveal the encircling forces. Her usual composure shows hairline cracks as she realizes the scope of what's arrayed against her. The energy dampening fields, quantum interference generators, and reality stabilization matrices being deployed represent technology specifically developed to counter her abilities\'97technology that suggests government research into weapons has advanced far beyond her expectations.\
\
\
\
\
\
The first UDF soldier to discover exactly how accurate Shakti\'92s perimeter guns were wanted to scratch his nose. He had been standing on a sandstone shelf for long enough that the itch had progressed from inconvenience to private campaign, but the last man who shifted his weight had received a bullet hole precisely where his boot had been heading. Their patrol had made good progress through the bush until the lead soldier stepped into a useful line of sight towards Sublime Point. A shot cracked. A neat hole appeared in the stone ahead of him. He stepped sideways; another hole appeared there. His sergeant moved to get a better look and acquired one between his feet. After that, six professional soldiers spent fifty-three minutes exploring the tactical possibilities of standing very still.\
Shakti security seemed happy with the arrangement. Attempts to edge backwards, forwards or sideways were answered with fresh little demonstrations of marksmanship; a surveillance camera vanished from one man\'92s pack without damaging either pack or man. Eventually, small lights began to wink through the trees behind them. The patrol followed, one cautious step at a time, while hidden speakers offered occasional corrections to anyone developing initiative. They emerged near their own lines carrying their weapons overhead and much of their dignity packed away for later. Nobody had been wounded. The UDF had, however, acquired useful information about the compound\'92s willingness to embarrass trespassers.\
Drones went up next. The first crossed a boundary only Shakti appeared able to see and became expensive confetti. A second came in low through the trees and achieved much the same result at shrub height. The third climbed, circled and survived long enough to transmit several seconds of excellent landscape before its component parts took separate routes home. Officers went back to their maps, patrol routes and calculations. Enough UDF personnel and equipment were settling around Sublime Point to stay there while the final decision on taking the place worked its way towards whoever needed to make it. Reconnaissance continued with growing respect for property lines.\
The tactical picture, stripped of acronyms and optimism, came down to two points:\
Yes, you probably can take this place.\
No, you are not going to enjoy doing it.\
Then a checkpoint stopped three white Continentals decorated for Christmas.\
\'93Purpose of visit?\'94\
\'93Christmas.\'94\
The soldier looked past the driver. Five more Elvi occupied the car along with tents, eskies, sleeping bags, two guitars and a plastic baby Jesus wedged beneath somebody\'92s elbow. Another Continental pulled up behind them. Then another. The Holy Momma was inside Sublime Point, Christmas was two sleeps away, and the One After the One was expected with an enthusiasm that had long since escaped the ordinary constraints of obstetrics. The Elvi had heard about the UDF too. All of it sounded worth turning up for.\
They came in vehicles representing several centuries of automotive optimism. Some Continentals and Cadillacs climbed without incident; others needed the sort of roadside encouragement normally reserved for livestock and relatives. One limousine coughed itself dead near the golf-course reaches of Sublime Point Road and was pushed onward by six Elvi while its driver steered with the solemn dignity of a man chauffeuring royalty. Another arrived behind a tow rope attached to a car of similar age and greater mechanical self-belief. The passengers in the disabled vehicle played guitar throughout. Mechanical failure ceased to qualify as a reason for missing Christmas.\
The golf course took the first serious overflow because some of its outer holes reached close towards Sublime Point, and because a surprising number of the people arriving turned out to know somebody whose parents were members. The club\'92s Christmas arrangements began with golfers and ended up negotiating tent placement with their children, their children\'92s friends and Elvi they had met that morning. Camping was tolerated on the rougher ground. Fairways and greens remained inviolate. The Elvi accepted this with complete seriousness and, having established the sacred boundaries of the campsite, discovered golf.\
They had brought guitars. Clubs had to be borrowed.\
A member\'92s son lent an Elvis a set. Another set appeared from somewhere else. Soon men who had come to celebrate a sacred birth were taking practice swings and offering each other technical advice acquired during the previous eleven minutes. By afternoon they had tee times, which they respected with greater discipline than several military checkpoints were managing with traffic. One performance in the clubhouse began late because the bass player was still on the back nine. Green fees were eventually absorbed into a show whose value became impossible to calculate once half the members\'92 children joined the audience.\
The UDF kept testing Sublime Point while the surrounding terrain acquired camping chairs. Another patrol tried an approach screened by thicker bush and spread itself out before reaching anything Shakti might consider provocative. One soldier lost the aerial from his communications pack. Another watched the sling detach from his rifle. A third stopped breathing when a branch six centimetres from his face severed itself cleanly and fell between his boots. Hidden speakers advised the patrol to follow the lights. They did. An Elvis on a nearby hole watched them file out of the bush with their weapons raised, missed a short putt and blamed the army.\
Portable toilets arrived before anyone had formally decided they were necessary. Folding chairs multiplied around the tents. Coolers appeared, then coffee carts, ice-cream vans, camping supplies, blankets, batteries, rain capes, portable chargers, food trucks, the CAKE, BAGEL AND ROLL van and two vehicles selling fried products whose biological origins remained protected by commercial confidence. People who had packed enough food for themselves met people who had packed enough for twenty. Wherever Elvi remained stationary for more than half an hour, commerce formed around them.\
Sarah\'92s device began filling with evidence. One photograph showed Elvi waving from a golf cart; another featured an Elvis in a bathrobe and one shoe standing beside a flagstick. Four men raised drinks beneath a handwritten HOLY MOMMA CHRISTMAS VIGIL AND CLOSEST-TO-THE-PIN sign. Someone sent a picture of two Elvi debating the break of a putt while a UDF checkpoint operated in the background. Another showed three guitars stacked beside an Elvis asleep under a table. Sarah forwarded that one to Hayley, who wanted to know whether the guitars were resting more comfortably than their owner.\
The Airborne children came to visit friends in the mountains, and friendship underwent the sort of rapid expansion usually associated with amnesties and very large inheritances. One kid knew somebody whose parents had a house nearby. Somebody else had access to a sixteen-bedroom mansion. Another knew the security code to a place whose owners had made the strategic error of leaving their son at home. Bedrooms filled first, followed by home theatres, billiard rooms, games rooms and pieces of furniture originally selected to demonstrate wealth rather than withstand sleeping Elvi.\
Parents accustomed to controlling access to mountains, clubs and one another\'92s social calendars watched unfamiliar young people cross their lawns carrying amplifiers. Their children opened side gates. Guitars appeared beside swimming pools, followed by Elvi in swimming pools, followed by towels provided by staff who had already decided resistance would create more work. At one house, a daughter came back from the golf club with a rhythm section because they looked cold. Three Elvi acquired the guest wing at another. A third mansion accumulated enough musicians for two bands, a jam session and a protracted argument about who had permission to move the keyboard.\
Commercial accommodation disappeared shortly afterwards. Hotels filled, then motels, then campgrounds. Spare rooms vanished into friendship networks of such complexity that hosts could identify neither their guests nor the Airborne child responsible for inviting them. An Elvis asleep on a billiard table acquired a blanket and a glass of water and remained there through breakfast, by which time everyone assumed he belonged to somebody.\
The Elvi carried the music into the village with them. Guitars came through the doors of one pub, then another, then the rest; amplifiers followed, someone found a piano, someone else tuned it, and by nightfall the younger residents had worked out which pub had the strongest band, which had the best harmonies, which had the strongest drinks, and which had somehow acquired three High Elvi and a drummer capable of holding a pint through an entire number. Groups migrated between venues. Airborne kids found more friends. Elvi borrowed equipment, played requests, signed things and disappeared into parties in houses whose owners would spend the following week reconstructing their guest lists from security footage.\
The UDF checkpoints processed all of this one car at a time. Tents, food, guitars, golf equipment, Airborne kids and Elvi kept presenting themselves to soldiers whose original brief had been considerably easier to understand.\
\'93Where are you staying?\'94\
\'93Dave\'92s.\'94\
\'93Who\'92s Dave?\'94\
The Elvis leaned out the window and shouted towards the car behind. \'93Who\'92s Dave?\'94\
Three people offered different answers.\
The soldier studied the queue stretching away behind them, handed back the documents and waved the car through.\
By the next evening, soldiers on checkpoint duty knew which Elvi were playing in which pubs, while the Elvi had learned which soldiers liked which songs. An outgoing corporal told his replacement that the Piglicious Minds lot were playing somewhere up the road. The incoming corporal checked his watch before he checked the next vehicle. Later, three UDF soldiers turned up at one of the performances still in uniform and had a thoroughly good time. A fourth found them there.\
Their sergeant arrived to retrieve them during the second set.\
He stayed for the encore.\

